## Required Design Sections

Produce a complete design document covering ALL of the following sections. Be specific and concrete — avoid vague generalizations.

### 1. Requirements Analysis
- Functional requirements (derived from use cases)
- Non-functional requirements (latency, throughput, availability, consistency)
- Capacity estimation (storage, bandwidth, compute — show your math)

### 2. High-Level Architecture
- System components and their responsibilities
- Data flow between components
- Technology choices with justification

### 3. Data Architecture
- Data model (entities, relationships, access patterns)
- Storage technology selection and rationale
- Consistency model (strong/eventual, CAP trade-offs)
- Partitioning and replication strategy

### 4. API Design
- Key endpoint definitions (method, path, request/response)
- Authentication and authorization scheme
- Rate limiting and pagination strategy
- Error response format

### 5. Scalability Strategy
- Horizontal vs. vertical scaling approach
- Caching strategy (what, where, invalidation)
- Async processing and queue design
- Database scaling (read replicas, sharding)

### 6. Reliability & Fault Tolerance
- Redundancy and replication
- Failure modes and recovery procedures
- Circuit breakers and graceful degradation
- Monitoring and alerting strategy

### 7. Security Design
- Authentication mechanism
- Authorization model (RBAC, ABAC)
- Data encryption (at rest, in transit)
- Threat model and mitigations

### 8. Trade-off Analysis
- Key design decisions and alternatives considered
- Trade-offs made and their rationale
- What you would do differently with more time/budget

